= The Library

Herein contains relevant information about the government and politics of the German republic.

@menu

- @government: Germany's system of government
- @weimar_timeline: Seeckt's Life before the Armstice of 1918 and his Return to Germany
- @curr_gov: Current government details
- @demographics: Demographics
- @parties: Parties
- @public_opinion: Public opinion (and projected election results)
- @paramilitaries: Paramilitary groups
- @factions: Internal factions
- @figures: Figures and charts
- @backSpecialScene: Exit library.

@government
go-to: menu

The German Republic (officially known as the German Reich), called by some of our detractors as the "Weimar Republic", after a term coined by Adolf Hitler, is a semi-presidential republic with a somewhat strong Reichspräsident, having the surpreme powers over the military, as well as extensive emergency powers. However the main power still lies with the parliament, called the Reichstag, which is controlled by the Reichsrat, the represantatives of the Länder. The Reichstag is responsible for governing, while the President has a largely ceremonial role but also has some important governing responsibilities.

The Reichstag is elected based on a proportional representation system, giving parties seats roughly proportional to their vote share in each electoral district. The Reichstag then elects the Chancellor, selected by the President and the cabinet by a majority vote, who are responsible for government. A majority vote-of-no-confidence can remove the current government.

The President is elected by a national direct vote, in two rounds. The second round is used if the first round does not result in a majority for one candidate; the candidate who receives a plurality in the second round becomes president (there is no obligation for weaker candidates to drop out). The role of the president is mostly to represent Germany, but he also has some crucial governing powers. The president and chancellor can together use emergency decrees to govern, bypassing the Reichstag. The president can also appoint or remove a chancellor when a parliamentary majority is unable to be established, and can thus influence the formation of a government.

Germany is divided into a number of states, the "Länder" each of which have their own governments with some responsibilities. By far the largest and most important state is Prussia.

At the time of the Game's Start, this Government System is not yet clearly established as the people are revolting all across the failing Empire...

@weimar_timeline
go-to: menu

= The Life of General Seeckt before the Game's Start

**1866**: On the 22. of April, Hans von Seeckt is born third child to prussian military officer Richard von Seeckt and his wife Augusta von Seeckt. 

**1885**: After graduating, Seeckt joins the Kaiser Alexander Garde-Grenadier-Regiment Nr.1 of the prussian army and starts to work his way up

**1896**: He continues to rise through the ranks after completing his general staff training, graduating amongst the top of his class.

**1915**: At the start of the War, Seeckt is lieutenant-colonel and general staff of the III. army-corps on the western front, before being relocated to the newly set up 11. Army on the east front, where he served under August von Mackensen. Together with Mackensen he wins several crucial battles, such as the Battle of Gorlice/Tarnów, for which he was awarded the Por-le-Mérite Order. In June of the same year. Seeckt was promoted to Generalmajor and in the following months, after the success of the serbian campaign, he was awarded oak leaves (eichenlaub) to his order pour-le-mérite.

**1916**: During the russian offensive of 1916, Seeckt briefly served as Chief of Staff of the 7. army.

**1917**: In December of 1917, Seeckt was sent to replace von Schellendorf as german counselor to and de-facto Chief of Staff of the Ottoman Empire. However, cooperation would prove dificult, as Enver Pascha, while willing to take counsel, would always disregard their opinion if it varied from his own. From his position he refused to condemn the genocide of armenians, arguing such measures were necesary to save Turkey from "internal decay".

**1918**: In the wake of inevitable defeat, Seeckt helped organizing the escape of the "Three Pashas", also known as the Young Turk triumvirate, before himself returning to Germany in November of 1918...

