Q.rb_strength = Math.round(Q.rb_strength);
Q.sh_strength = Math.round(Q.sh_strength);
Q.sa_strength = Math.round(Q.sa_strength); 
!}
go-to: menu

**Paramilitary groups:**

**Reichsbanner Schwarz-Rot-Gold**: The Reichsbanner is an organization affiliated with all of the democratic parties - the SPD, Z, and DDP, but primarily with the SPD. It was formed in February 1924, and although technically a paramilitary, it is an unarmed and rather ceremonial organization.

Strength: [+ rb_strength +] thousand

Militarization: [+ rb_militancy : militancy +]

**Div. Communist Paramiltaries such as: Roter Soldatenbund (RSB), Rote Ruhrarmeel, Proletarische Hundertschaften, Rotfrontkämpferbund (RFB)**: These Comunist Groups are associated with the KPD-SB and were born out of the German Revolution, opposing both the Kaiserreich and the newly formed Bourgeois Republic.

Strength: [+ rfb_strength +] thousand

Militarization: [+ rfb_militancy : militancy +]

**Der Stahlhelm**: A right-wing nationalist organization of primarily Great War veterans, founded on the 25. December 1918, descended from the *Freikorps* that emerged after the war. Lead by a "Führer" it is associated with the DNVP.

Strength: [+ sh_strength +] thousand

Militarization: [+ sh_militancy : militancy +]

**Sturmabteilung (SA)**: The nazi paramilitary group. Founded in November of 1920 as the sports- and gymnastics-section of the DAP, it was only renamed to it's, now well known name, on the 5. of October 1921. It consists of Völkisch Freikorps and Reichswehr soldiers.

Strength: [+ sa_strength +] thousand

Militarization: [+ sa_militancy : militancy +] 

**Freikorps**: Scattered groups of primarily demobilised soldiers and young nationalists. They vary strongly in their political Opinions, ranging from the national liberal "Offiziers-Kompanie-Cassel" (later Jungdeutscher Orden), lead by Arthur Mahraun, to the völkisch nationalist Freikorps Oberland, who supported the Beer Hall Putsch and later merged into the SA.

**Official military/paramilitary groups:**

**Reichswehr**: [+ reichswehr_strength +] thousand troops. This is the military of the German Reich. As *Chef des Truppenamtes*, it is your task to rebuild and restructure it after the devastating losses of the Great War.

Loyalty: [+ reichswehr_loyalty : loyalty +]

**Prussian police**: [+ prussian_police_strength +] thousand members.

Loyalty: [+ prussian_police_loyalty : loyalty +]



@factions
go-to: neorevisionists if neorevisionism > 0; menu if neorevisionism <= 0

**Internal factions of the Reichswehr**

Overall dissent: [+ dissent_percent : dissent +]

// ludendorffist-left, conservatives-center, reformers-reformists, pragmatist-labor
**Ludendorffists**

The Ludendorffists, centered around General Ludendorff and his ideas are strongly opposed to the reforms anticipated by the reformers around Seeckt and Groener, while supporting their own reform agenda, aiming to transform the army into a "Volksarmee" incooperating all germans eligible for service. They are strongly national revanchist, believing in a racial war to "clean" the european continent and assert German dominance over it's neighbours.

Strength: [+ left_strength : strength +]

Dissent: [+ left_dissent : dissent +]

**Conservatives**

The conservative clique in the Reichswehr is strongly oriented around pre-great war doctrine and sceptical of all reform, while tolerating Seeckt. They are personally opposed to the Republic and would like to see an authocratic ruler in it's place (preferably the Kaiser). They uphold prussian traditions and believe the army should follow Moltke's and Clausewitz footsteps.

Strength: [+ center_strength : strength +]

Dissent: [+ center_dissent : dissent +]

**Pragmatists**

The pragmatists are the biggest faction in the reichswehr, but also the most diverse and broadly described one, ranging from repunlicans by conviction to bavarian regionalists. It is mostly apolitical and generally supportive of army reforms, although certain groups withhin this faction may swing either way, depending on the question.

Strength: [+ labor_strength : strength +]

Dissent: [+ labor_dissent : dissent +]

**Reformers**

Reformers, centered around Seeckt and Groener, are in most cases disapproving, but pragmatic regarding their stance on the Republic and are standing fully behind Seeckt and his ambitions to reform the Reichswehr into a politically independent, professional army. They operate in the spirit of Mackensen's Blitzkrieg and Moltke's Auftragstaktik, while adhering to the requirements of politics.

Strength: [+ reformist_strength : strength +]

Dissent: [+ reformist_dissent : dissent +]

**Republicans**

Republican behaviour was strongly discouraged in the german army as long as the war was ongoing. However, with the november revolution, republicans and socialist gained traction withhin the army, only to be eiher discharged or to resign after the Kapp-Putsch. While their most important member is Reinhardt, mostly lower ranked soldiers are part of it.

@neorevisionists
go-to: menu

**Neorevisionists**

Neorevisionists focus on defending the republic and expanding democracy, while also expanding the party's base beyond the working class to create a people's party. They support new propaganda methods and extraparliamentary action in defense of the republic.

Strength: [+ neorevisionist_strength : strength +]

Dissent: [+ neorevisionist_dissent : dissent +]


